# openFDA key — free: https://open.fda.gov/apis/authentication/
# Edge Function secret for `nrt-search`, same rules as above: server-side only,
# never reaches the client. Optional to run and required in practice — without
# it openFDA allows 1,000 requests a day *per IP*, and Edge Functions egress
# from shared addresses, so an anonymous deployment spends a budget it does not
# own. With a key it is 120,000 a day, counted against the key rather than the
# address. Leave blank for local runs and tests; set it before deploying:
# `supabase secrets set OPENFDA_API_KEY=…`
OPENFDA_API_KEY=

# Supabase, for the app. Both are the opposite case to the key above: they are
# *designed* to ship inside the client build, and auth and RLS protect the data
# rather than the secrecy of these strings. Copy the real values from Project
# Settings → API Keys; use the publishable key (`sb_publishable_…`), not the
# legacy anon JWT, which is compatibility-only.
#
# The EXPO_PUBLIC_ prefix outlived Expo. apps/ios/Scripts/write-config.sh reads
# these names into the iOS build, and renaming them means changing the script,
# project.yml, AppConfiguration and the UI tests together.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Optional, and only device builds need it: the Apple team to sign with.
#
# Read it from the profile Xcode creates, not from the keychain:
#
#   security cms -D -i ~/Library/Developer/Xcode/UserData/Provisioning\ Profiles/*.mobileprovision \
#     | plutil -extract TeamIdentifier.0 raw -
#
# `security find-identity` also prints a code in parentheses, and it is the
# team of *that certificate* — which may belong to an account that has since
# been replaced. Taking it from there put a stale id in this file and cost a
# build that failed with "No Account for Team".
#
# The simulator ignores this; a phone will not build without it, and
# `xcodegen generate` rewrites the project file — so choosing the team in
# Xcode's UI lasts only until the next regeneration.
TAPER_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=
